  "verdict": "void",
  "section": "119",
  "quote": "A landlord who is lawfully entitled to increase the rent charged to a tenant for a rental unit may do so only if at least 12 months have elapsed",
  "reason": "Section 119(1) of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 prohibits a landlord from increasing the rent unless at least 12 months have elapsed since the day of the last rent increase or the day the rental unit was first rented to the tenant. Because this clause attempts to allow rent increases every six months, it is inconsistent with section 119 and is therefore void under section 4(1) of the Act."
}

